insert into prefecture_texts (prefecture_code, lang, body)
values
(1, E'en', E'Hokkaido is Japan\'s great northern wilderness — and its pantry. Summer brings the lavender fields of Furano and the hills of Biei; winter means the Sapporo Snow Festival and some of the world\'s best powder snow. Spot wild animals in Shiretoko, then hit Hakodate for its night view and morning market. Seafood bowls, jingisukan lamb BBQ and miso ramen are reasons to visit on their own.'),
(2, E'en', E'Aomori is the land of the Nebuta Festival and Tsugaru shamisen. Walk the Oirase Gorge trail, cruise Lake Towada, and see Hirosaki Castle — one of Japan\'s finest cherry-blossom spots. Winter is just as rich, with the snow monsters of Hakkoda and the Sukayu hot spring. Don\'t miss Oma tuna and apple sweets. Best in cherry season and during August\'s Nebuta.'),
(3, E'en', E'Iwate is quiet history country, home to the golden hall of Chuson-ji in Hiraizumi. The white cliffs of Jodogahama, the underground lake of Ryusendo cave and the folklore village of Tono dot the prefecture. Try the "three great noodles" of Morioka — wanko soba, reimen and jajamen. Deep Tohoku, without the crowds. Aim for fresh green or autumn-leaf season.'),
(4, E'en', E'Miyagi pairs the "city of trees" Sendai with sea and mountains. Cruise the pine islands of Matsushima — one of Japan\'s three great views — stroll the castle town of the samurai lord Date Masamune, and peer into the crater lake of Zao. August brings the famous Tanabata festival. Gyutan (beef tongue), zunda mochi and Sanriku sushi are the local table.'),
(5, E'en', E'Akita is the land of hidden hot springs — Nyuto Onsen above all — and the Namahage demons. Japan\'s deepest lake Tazawa, the samurai street of Kakunodate and the snow huts of Yokote pack it with snow-country charm. Kiritanpo hotpot, Inaniwa udon and superb sake round it out. Beautiful under Kakunodate\'s cherry blossoms, and in open-air baths in the snow.'),
(6, E'en', E'Yamagata means the 1,000 stone steps of Yamadera temple and the snow monsters of Zao. The gaslit streets of Ginzan Onsen are at their most beautiful on a snowy night. Summer is cherry-picking season; autumn brings imoni stew parties on the riverbanks. Yonezawa beef and chilled ramen are the local specialities. A prefecture for quiet hot springs and snow scenery.'),
(7, E'en', E'Fukushima is castle-town Aizu and the Bandai highlands. See Tsuruga Castle and samurai residences, the Edo-period post town of Ouchi-juku, and the blue volcanic ponds of Goshiki-numa. Kitakata is famous for morning ramen; try the enban gyoza too. Superb autumn leaves in Urabandai and atmospheric snow in winter. Surprisingly quiet for somewhere so close to Tokyo.'),
(8, E'en', E'Ibaraki is home to Hitachi Seaside Park, where whole hills turn blue with nemophila in spring and red with kochia in autumn. Kairakuen — one of Japan\'s three great gardens — Fukuroda Falls and the giant Ushiku Buddha anchor the sights. From the Pacific come anglerfish hotpot and Hitachi beef; this is also natto\'s homeland. Time your visit for the flowers (April–May, October).'),
(9, E'en', E'Tochigi holds the gilded splendour of Nikko Toshogu beside the natural beauty of Lake Chuzenji and Kegon Falls. Soak in Kinugawa Onsen, then hop between gyoza shops in Utsunomiya. Ashikaga Flower Park is famous for its great wisteria (late April–May) and winter illuminations. For a World Heritage site and hot springs in one trip, start here.'),
(10, E'en', E'Gunma is hot-spring country: Kusatsu, Ikaho and Shima. Watch sulphurous steam rise from Kusatsu\'s yubatake, then climb Ikaho\'s stone steps with an onsen manju in hand. The World Heritage Tomioka Silk Mill and the Tanigawadake ropeway round out the sights. Mizusawa udon and yaki-manju are the local bites. Best in snow season or fresh early summer.'),
(11, E'en', E'Saitama\'s calling card is Kawagoe, "Little Edo", with its clay-walled warehouse streets. Add the wind chimes of Hikawa Shrine, boat rides down the Nagatoro rapids and the pink moss fields of Chichibu. The Chichibu Night Festival in December is one of Japan\'s three great float festivals. Eel and Musashino udon are the specialities — and Edo is only 30 minutes from Tokyo.'),
(12, E'en', E'Chiba is the temple town of Naritasan Shinsho-ji and the long Kujukuri coast. At Cape Inubosaki lighthouse you can catch Honshu\'s earliest sunrise. Hike Yoro Gorge, then eat your way through the fishing ports of Kamogawa and Katsuura. Simmered kinmedai (golden-eye snapper) and Katsuura tantanmen are the dishes to order. Fishing-town air, right next to Tokyo.'),
(13, E'en', E'Tokyo is one of the world\'s great megacities — yet old shitamachi lanes survive a few blocks from the neon. Senso-ji and its Nakamise arcade, the forest of Meiji Shrine and the Shibuya scramble crossing swap eras every few train stops. It has more Michelin stars than any city on earth: sushi, ramen, tempura. However many times you come, there is always a first-time Tokyo waiting.'),
(14, E'en', E'Kanagawa wears three faces: the port city of Yokohama, the old capital of Kamakura and the hot springs of Hakone. See the Great Buddha and ride the Enoden line along the sea, cruise Lake Ashi past Owakudani\'s volcanic steam, then graze through Yokohama\'s Chinatown. Mt Fuji over Lake Ashi is sharpest on clear winter days. Sea, mountains and onsen, one hour from Tokyo.'),
(15, E'en', E'Niigata is Japan\'s rice-and-sake heartland, with about 90 breweries — the most of any prefecture — and a tasting hall right inside the station. Summer means Sado Island\'s clear water and tub-boat rides; winter means superb snow at Naeba and Yuzawa. Hegi soba, nodoguro rosy seabass and Tochio\'s giant fried tofu are the local table. The Nagaoka fireworks in August are once-in-a-lifetime.'),
(16, E'en', E'Toyama is famous for the "snow corridor" of the Tateyama Kurobe Alpine Route, its walls of snow open April–June. Three-thousand-metre peaks rise straight from the bay, and from Amaharashi Coast you can see them across the water. Toyama Bay\'s white shrimp, firefly squid and winter yellowtail are exceptionally fresh. Kansui Park, home of "the world\'s most beautiful Starbucks", is a favourite too.'),
(17, E'en', E'Ishikawa is Kanazawa: Kenrokuen garden, geisha teahouse districts and a deep craft tradition. Stroll Higashi Chaya with gold-leaf ice cream, then take in the 21st Century Museum of Contemporary Art. Try your hand at Wajima lacquer or Kutani porcelain. The seafood bowls and nodoguro of Omicho market are essential eating. Winter, when Kenrokuen wears its snow ropes, is quietly beautiful.'),
(18, E'en', E'Fukui means the sheer cliffs of Tojinbo and dinosaurs — its Dinosaur Museum ranks among the world\'s three best and is a hit with families. At Eihei-ji, the head temple of Soto Zen, you can watch the silent morning service. Echizen crab (winter), grated-daikon soba and sauce katsudon are the dishes. Few tourists come — which means the quiet Hokuriku coast is all yours.'),
(19, E'en', E'Yamanashi owns the classic views of Mt Fuji: the reflection on Lake Kawaguchi and the five-storey pagoda of Arakurayama Sengen Park. Katsunuma is Japan\'s wine country, and houtou noodle hotpot and peach picking fill out the menu. Camping and sauna culture thrive around the Fuji Five Lakes. Late autumn to winter, when the air is clearest, is Fuji\'s best season.'),
(20, E'en', E'Nagano is Japan\'s mountain kingdom, ringed by 3,000-metre peaks. Walk the Azusa River at Kamikochi, watch snow monkeys soak in hot springs at Jigokudani, and visit Matsumoto Castle and Zenko-ji temple. Summer is for trekking and cool air; winter for Hakuba and Nozawa\'s slopes and snowy open-air baths. Shinshu soba, nozawana pickles and apples are the staples. If you love mountains, start here.'),
(21, E'en', E'Gifu holds the thatched villages of Shirakawa-go and the castle town of Hida Takayama. Graze on Hida beef sushi in the old streets, then browse the morning market. Gero is one of Japan\'s three famous hot springs, and cormorant fishing on the Nagara River (May–October) is a 1,300-year-old summer rite. The snowy light-up of Shirakawa-go is a winter-only reward.'),
(22, E'en', E'Shizuoka sits between Mt Fuji and Suruga Bay — tea and wasabi country. See Fuji from the pine grove of Miho no Matsubara, walk tea fields at Nihondaira, and follow the Izu coast from onsen to onsen. Sakura shrimp, shirasu, eel and Shizuoka oden fill the table. Atami and Shuzenji are a short shinkansen hop from Tokyo. An open-air bath with Fuji in view is hard to beat.'),
(23, E'en', E'Aichi is the home of "Nagoya meshi": miso katsu, hitsumabushi eel, chicken wings and a famous breakfast culture. Nagoya Castle with its golden dolphins, the Tokugawa Art Museum and Ghibli Park lead the sights. The little art island of Sakushima in Mikawa Bay is quietly gaining fans. A city-walking, eat-everything kind of trip suits it best.'),
(24, E'en', E'Mie is the prefecture of Ise Jingu, the destination of the classic once-in-a-lifetime pilgrimage — with the food stalls of Okage Yokocho as part of the fun. See sunrise between the wedded rocks of Meoto Iwa, meet the ama divers of Toba and Shima, and cruise Ago Bay\'s ria coast. Matsusaka beef, spiny lobster and tekone sushi are first-rate. A crisp winter-morning visit to the shrine is unforgettable.'),
(25, E'en', E'Shiga wraps around Lake Biwa, Japan\'s largest lake. A torii gate floats offshore at Shirahige Shrine; national-treasure Hikone Castle and the monastery of Mt Hiei watch over the water. Omi beef is Japan\'s oldest wagyu brand. Cyclists come from all over for the "Biwaichi" loop around the lake. All this calm is just ten minutes by train from Kyoto.'),
(26, E'en', E'Kyoto was Japan\'s capital for a thousand years. The vermilion tunnel of Fushimi Inari, the gold of Kinkaku-ji, the stage of Kiyomizu — the list of famous sights never ends. Cherry blossom and autumn leaves are peerless here, and peak crowds come with them; go early to the bamboo grove or late to Pontocho like a local. From yudofu to kaiseki to matcha sweets, even the food is elegant.'),
(27, E'en', E'Osaka is Japan\'s eating capital. Under the neon of Dotonbori and the Glico sign, hop from takoyaki to okonomiyaki to kushikatsu. Osaka Castle, Tsutenkaku tower and Universal Studios Japan cover the daytime. The city\'s friendly, talkative streak is unmatched in Japan. Come here for long nights, laughter and food.'),
(28, E'en', E'Hyogo is home to Himeji Castle, the White Heron — a World Heritage site. Add the cosmopolitan streets of Kobe, the "ten-million-dollar" night view from Mt Rokko and the seven public baths of Kinosaki Onsen. Kobe beef, akashiyaki and Nada sake are essential. Arima Onsen, beloved by the shogun Hideyoshi, is among Japan\'s oldest springs. City, castle and onsen in one trip.'),
(29, E'en', E'Nara is the old capital of the Great Buddha and the bowing deer. Todai-ji\'s vast Buddha hall, deer-filled Nara Park and the vermilion Kasuga Taisha make the classic circuit. Go further for Yoshino, a whole mountain of cherry blossoms in April, or the ancient Yamanobe road. Persimmon-leaf sushi and kudzu sweets are the local taste. Fewer crowds than Kyoto — and an older Japan.'),
(30, E'en', E'Wakayama is pilgrimage country: the Kumano Kodo trails and the monastery mountain of Koyasan. Walk cedar-lined paths, sleep in temple lodgings and join the morning prayers. The sight of Nachi Falls beside the vermilion pagoda is breathtaking. Shirahama\'s white beach and pandas, and Katsuura\'s tuna, round it out. For walkers and soul-restorers.'),
(31, E'en', E'Tottori has Japan\'s largest sand dunes — ride a camel, try sandboarding, then see the carved masterpieces of the Sand Museum. The yokai bronze statues of Mizuki Shigeru Road and the white-walled storehouses of Kurayoshi add character. Winter matsuba crab and 20th-century pears are the tastes. It is one of Japan\'s least-crowded prefectures — and that is the luxury.'),
(32, E'en', E'Shimane is home to Izumo Taisha, the grand shrine of matchmaking where the gods of Japan are said to gather. Watch the sunset over Lake Shinji, tour the World Heritage Iwami Ginzan silver mine, and see Japan\'s finest garden at the Adachi Museum. Izumo soba and shijimi clam soup are the staples. Visiting in Kamiarizuki — the month the gods arrive — is special.'),
(33, E'en', E'Okayama, the "Land of Sunshine", is the white walls and willows of the Kurashiki Bikan quarter, plus Korakuen — one of Japan\'s three great gardens — and Crow Castle. It is also the gateway to the art islands of Inujima and Teshima. White peaches, muscat grapes and barazushi live up to the fruit-kingdom name. With little rain year-round, it rarely disappoints.'),
(34, E'en', E'Hiroshima holds two World Heritage sites: the A-Bomb Dome and Itsukushima Shrine. Miyajima\'s great torii shows a different face at high and low tide. Okonomiyaki, oysters and Onomichi ramen make it a food heavyweight too. Cycling the Shimanami Kaido puts the island-dotted Inland Sea under your own two wheels. A trip that faces history and is healed by the sea.'),
(35, E'en', E'Yamaguchi, at Honshu\'s western tip, is a run of photogenic landscapes: the five arches of Kintaikyo bridge, the red torii rows of Motonosumi Shrine — picked by CNN among Japan\'s most beautiful places — and the karst plateau of Akiyoshidai. Shimonoseki is the true home of fugu. In Hagi\'s castle town you can trace the footsteps of the Meiji Restoration. Quiet, deep and for connoisseurs.'),
(36, E'en', E'Tokushima is the Awa Odori and the Naruto whirlpools. In August the whole city becomes a dance floor. The vine bridges and deep gorges of the Iya Valley rank among Japan\'s three hidden regions. See the whirlpools from a boat or the glass-floored walkway. Tokushima ramen, sudachi citrus and Naruto sea bream feed you well. Summer festival plus secret valleys — that\'s the pairing.'),
(37, E'en', E'Kagawa is the "Udon Prefecture" — pilgrims hop between noodle mills from breakfast. Naoshima and Teshima, stages of the Setouchi Triennale, are world-famous art islands. Ritsurin Garden holds three Michelin stars, and Konpira-san\'s 785 stone steps are a rite of passage. Japan\'s smallest prefecture, packed tight with food and art.'),
(38, E'en', E'Ehime means Dogo Onsen, said to have inspired Spirited Away — bathe in the grand old bathhouse, then stroll the arcades in a yukata. Matsuyama Castle is one of only twelve original keeps; the Shimanami islands and the terraced shores of the Uwa Sea lie beyond. Tai-meshi sea bream rice and mikan are the true local taste. A literary city too, from Soseki to Shiki.'),
(39, E'en', E'Kochi is the Shimanto, Japan\'s "last clear stream" — cycle over its low-water bridges and canoe its length. The Yosakoi festival in August, the Sunday market and Sakamoto Ryoma\'s statue at Katsurahama fill out the classics. Straw-seared katsuo tataki is a different dish here — the aroma alone. Kashiwajima\'s water rivals Okinawa\'s. A summer prefecture of big food and river play.'),
(40, E'en', E'Fukuoka is the city of yatai — riverside food stalls in Nakasu where you hop from tonkotsu ramen to yaki-ramen late into the night. Day trips are rich too: the plum groves of Dazaifu Tenmangu, seaside cafés in Itoshima, canal boats in Yanagawa. Motsunabe, mentaiko and udon give it Kyushu\'s deepest food bench. The airport is five minutes from downtown by subway.'),
(41, E'en', E'Saga is the birthplace of Arita and Imari porcelain — walk the kiln towns, throw a pot, and shop for tableware. At Yobuko\'s morning market the squid sashimi is served still translucent. The cliff-hung Yutoku Inari Shrine and November\'s international balloon festival are the other draws. Ureshino and Takeo are fine hot springs too. A prefecture for people who love ceramics and food.'),
(42, E'en', E'Nagasaki is a port city of slopes and churches. Glover Garden, Oura Cathedral and the night view from Mt Inasa — ranked among the world\'s three best — are the classics. Cruise to Gunkanjima and step onto the abandoned island. Champon, sara-udon and castella reflect its history as Japan\'s only open port. Its tram-and-hills exoticism exists nowhere else in Japan.'),
(43, E'en', E'Kumamoto is the great caldera of Aso and Kumamoto Castle. Horses graze the grasslands of Kusasenri, and the Daikanbo lookout floats above a sea of clouds. The castle\'s restoration and the crystal springs of Shirakawa are worth the detour. Basashi horse sashimi, akaushi beef bowls and karashi renkon are the table. Add the open-air baths of Kurokawa Onsen for nature, castle and hot springs in one.'),
(44, E'en', E'Oita, the self-declared "Onsen Prefecture", leads Japan in both spring sources and hot-water volume. In Beppu, tour the boiling "hells" and get buried in hot sand; in Yufuin, catch the morning mist on Lake Kinrin and browse the refined spa town. Toriten fried chicken, seki-aji and seki-saba mackerel and dango-jiru feed you between baths. If hot springs are the point of the trip, this is the summit.'),
(45, E'en', E'Miyazaki is myth and subtropics. Row a boat beneath the Manai Falls in Takachiho Gorge — said to be where the gods descended — and it will stay with you. The "devil\'s washboard" rocks of Aoshima, wild horses at Cape Toi and the Nichinan coast drive are pure pleasure. Chicken nanban, charcoal-grilled jidori and mangoes are the tastes. Mild in winter, surfable all year.'),
(46, E'en', E'Kagoshima is Satsuma, where the Sakurajima volcano smokes across the bay. Only here can you be buried in naturally heated sand at Ibusuki while watching an active volcano. The ancient cedars of World Heritage Yakushima and the seas of Amami are in reach. Kurobuta pork shabu-shabu, satsuma-age and imo shochu are the flavours. Rich Meiji Restoration history makes it endlessly interesting.'),
(47, E'en', E'Okinawa is the old Ryukyu Kingdom and some of the world\'s finest ocean. Feel the islands\' spirituality at Shuri Castle and Sefa-utaki, then snorkel and dive the Kerama, Miyako and Ishigaki waters. Okinawa soba, goya champuru and awamori make a food culture of its own. The sea is best May–October, but winters stay around 20°C. Another country, inside Japan.')
